<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Paris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1150–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> was one of the first universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, emerging in the twelfth century from the cathedral schools of Paris and receiving royal and papal privileges around 1200. It was the leading centre of theology and philosophy in medieval Europe, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus">Albertus Magnus</a> taught. Its college of the Sorbonne gave it a popular name, and it was divided into separate universities in 1970.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-paris.jpg" alt="University of Paris" width="320" height="352" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coat_of_arms_of_the_University_of_Paris.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
